---
import Layout from '../layouts/Layout.astro';
import { sources } from '../data/demo';
import { ArrowUpRight, Info, Database, ShieldCheck } from '@lucide/astro';
---
<Layout title="Data Sources & Methodology" active="methodology"><div class="page-shell subpage"><div class="page-intro"><div><span class="eyebrow">TRANSPARENT BY DESIGN</span><h1>Know what <span>you're looking at.</span></h1><p>The sources, the distinctions, and the limits behind the numbers.</p></div></div><div class="content-notice"><Info size={20} /><div><strong>This MVP uses illustrative data.</strong><p>All dashboard numbers, chart series, probability changes, and the ocean color field are synthetic examples. The date labels are examples, not verified publication dates. No live NOAA feed is connected.</p></div></div><section class="method-section" id="indices"><span class="eyebrow">01 / THE INDICATORS</span><h2>Different numbers answer different questions.</h2><div class="index-grid"><article><span class="index-label">WEEKLY OBSERVATION</span><h3>Niño 3.4 anomaly</h3><p>A sea surface temperature anomaly for a tropical Pacific region, relative to a reference climatology. A weekly reading is not an official ENSO phase declaration.</p></article><article><span class="index-label">SEASONAL RELATIVE INDEX</span><h3>RONI</h3><p>The Relative Oceanic Niño Index accounts for tropical ocean background warming and uses a three-month window. It is not directly interchangeable with a weekly Niño 3.4 anomaly.</p></article><article><span class="index-label">HISTORICAL INDEX</span><h3>ONI</h3><p>The Oceanic Niño Index is a conventional three-month Niño 3.4 anomaly. Comparisons should state the index, reference period, observation window, and data version.</p></article></div></section><section class="method-section"><span class="eyebrow">02 / ORIGINAL PUBLICATIONS</span><h2>Go back to the source.</h2><div class="source-list">{sources.map(source => <a href={source.href} target="_blank" rel="noreferrer"><Database size={19} /><div><strong>{source.name}</strong><span>{source.type}</span></div><ArrowUpRight size={17} /></a>)}</div><p class="figure-note">Land outlines in the illustrative Pacific map: Natural Earth, public domain.</p></section><section class="method-section"><span class="eyebrow">03 / EDITORIAL BOUNDARIES</span><h2>Context with its limits intact.</h2><div class="explanation-grid"><div><h3>Probability is not impact severity.</h3><p>An ENSO phase probability is not a probability of flooding, snowfall, crop damage, or a hurricane landfall. Historical regional tendencies are not current regional forecasts.</p></div><div><h3>Revisions should remain visible.</h3><p>A production data pipeline should preserve issue dates, target seasons, observation periods, source URLs, retrieval times, and revisions. Stale observations should remain clearly dated.</p></div></div></section><div class="source-callout"><ShieldCheck size={22} /><div><strong>Independent, with original sources one click away.</strong><p>ENSOLens is not affiliated with NOAA or CPC. This preview has no analytics, advertising, accounts, or email collection.</p></div></div></div></Layout>
